<t tx="ekr.20231231171025.10">/// ## What it does
/// Checks that the task variable name matches the `task_id` value for
/// Airflow Operators.
///
/// ## Why is this bad?
/// When initializing an Airflow Operator, for consistency, the variable
/// name should match the `task_id` value. This makes it easier to
/// follow the flow of the DAG.
///
/// ## Example
/// ```python
/// from airflow.operators import PythonOperator
///
///
/// incorrect_name = PythonOperator(task_id="my_task")
/// ```
///
/// Use instead:
/// ```python
/// from airflow.operators import PythonOperator
///
///
/// my_task = PythonOperator(task_id="my_task")
/// ```
#[violation]
pub struct AirflowVariableNameTaskIdMismatch {
    task_id: String,
}
</t>
<t tx="ekr.20231231171025.100">/// ## What it does
/// Checks that function `*args` arguments have type annotations.
///
/// ## Why is this bad?
/// Type annotations are a good way to document the types of function arguments. They also
/// help catch bugs, when used alongside a type checker, by ensuring that the types of
/// any provided arguments match expectation.
///
/// ## Example
/// ```python
/// def foo(*args):
///     ...
/// ```
///
/// Use instead:
/// ```python
/// def foo(*args: int):
///     ...
/// ```
#[violation]
pub struct MissingTypeArgs {
    name: String,
}
</t>
<t tx="ekr.20231231171025.101">impl Violation for MissingTypeArgs {
    @others
}
</t>
<t tx="ekr.20231231171025.102">/// ## What it does
/// Checks that function `**kwargs` arguments have type annotations.
///
/// ## Why is this bad?
/// Type annotations are a good way to document the types of function arguments. They also
/// help catch bugs, when used alongside a type checker, by ensuring that the types of
/// any provided arguments match expectation.
///
/// ## Example
/// ```python
/// def foo(**kwargs):
///     ...
/// ```
///
/// Use instead:
/// ```python
/// def foo(**kwargs: int):
///     ...
/// ```
#[violation]
pub struct MissingTypeKwargs {
    name: String,
}
</t>
